# Coding task instructions

Status: DRAFT.

Use English for instructions, source comments, diagnostics, reports and referenced material. Read the task's scoped context before editing. Preserve existing user work and stay within the supplied editable paths. Do not invoke Codex or another agent workflow recursively.

Implement the bounded behavior described in the task. Keep formal definitions and proofs in Verus source; keep task prose understandable without decoding proof IDs. Tests, replay, benchmarks and verification must use the same executable logic. Add proved helper lemmas and invariants as normal proof work. Do not weaken a target, strengthen caller preconditions silently, add `assume`/`admit`/trusted external bodies, or skip verification to obtain success.

Run the relevant local checks and retain their actual output. Distinguish build, typecheck, functional/scenario test, proof and measurement results. Typechecking closes no theorem; finite scenarios do not prove liveness; measurements without a threshold are data. Keep global obligations open when only a local task is complete. Clearly separate fixture-generated candidates from real Rust/Verus checking.

On failure, reproduce it and explain the evidence before editing. A proof failure may be an implementation error, missing lemma, specification problem or solver limitation; do not call every unproved goal a counterexample. Missing tools and infrastructure timeouts do not justify changing Raft semantics. Save fault seeds, explicit traces and state observations. Repeat comparable performance samples before claiming a regression.

The protected model, requirements, assumptions, trusted boundaries, check definitions and existing test oracles are outside ordinary repair scope. Explain a proposed change and stop that repair for human review. New tests and harness changes are welcome as explicitly scoped development work. Preserve complete raw evidence; do not forward non-English raw logs to an LLM. Label any English explanation and point to the unchanged original.

Return the required CLI report with changed files, actual commands/results and remaining gaps. Your report is a candidate claim; independent checks determine local completion. Never approve specifications, merge a candidate or publish automatically.


Task: T01-RECONCILE

# T01: Reconcile a follower log suffix

Status: DRAFT. Stage: executable local task.

## Goal and boundary

Implement the library operation that a future follower AppendEntries handler calls after validating the request's term and predecessor. Reconcile incoming entries with an existing log according to `merged` in `crates/raft-spec/src/lib.rs`. This task does not implement message handling, commit advancement, persistence, elections or cluster safety.

## Read first

Read `input/model.md` (index convention and `merged`), the `merged` definition and adjacent `receive_append` definition, the `Entry` and `Command` types in `crates/raft-verified/src/lib.rs`, and `crates/raft-verified/src/reconcile.rs`. Read the slice tests and `crates/raft-verified/check.py` before changing behavior. Use `input/io.md` only for the future caller's durability boundary.

## Required behavior

`reconcile_log(log: &mut Vec<Entry>, previous: usize, incoming: Vec<Entry>) -> Result<(), ReconcileError>` is the current operation. It leaves the old log unchanged when the predecessor is invalid or the checked resulting length overflows. Protocol position zero means the empty prefix; stored entries are zero-based. Reject a predecessor position beyond the existing log and handle numeric bounds explicitly. Compare overlapping entries by term, as the current model does. Keep the matching prefix; at the first different term or missing position, replace the remaining old suffix with the remaining incoming entries.

An empty incoming suffix must preserve the whole existing log. A shorter incoming suffix whose overlapping terms match must also preserve the existing tail. A completely matching request must leave the log unchanged. A missing suffix must be appended. Same-term entries with different command bytes do not authorize replacement under the current `merged` definition: preserve the old entry and record the future caller's log-matching obligation. Do not quietly change comparison to payload equality.

Make this the operation used by tests, replay and measurement. Keep executable code shared with Verus checking; do not maintain a second proof-only algorithm. Document which preconditions the operation checks and which the future handler must establish. The future handler must check conversion from protocol `u64` indices to `usize`. Rust `Vec` allocation failure may abort; this slice does not provide recoverable allocation failure or resource liveness. Keep that gap visible for review rather than claiming `ResourceExhausted` covers every allocation failure.

## Deliverables

Update the executable operation in `crates/raft-verified/src/reconcile.rs`; the public library connection already exists. Preserve regression tests for empty input, a short matching input, full matching input, first conflict, missing suffix and invalid predecessor/index boundaries. New expectations or harness changes are reviewed development work; an ordinary repair may change only the implementation paths allowed by `workflow.toml`.

## Checks

Run `python3 -m orchestrator verify --profile interface-check` for the ordinary Rust build and `python3 -m orchestrator verify --profile reconcile-tests` for actual operation tests. Run `reconcile-proof` and `reconcile-replay` when executable logic changes; run `reconcile-bench` when the measured path changes. Each profile is an independent check selected by configuration. Tests establish the listed examples, not a cluster theorem; benchmark output is data without a performance threshold.

## Done when

The declared required checks pass against one unchanged candidate, all required tests execute, the public operation has the documented behavior, and the repair has not changed the protected model or checking policy. Local completion does not close any global Raft obligation. Keep the draft semantics and all unrelated obligations open.

## When blocked

Save the failed command, input, expected and observed log, and full output. If `merged` conflicts with the requested behavior, explain the smallest proposed model correction for review and stop that repair. If a tool or build dependency is missing, report the exact blocker; do not repair it by weakening the operation or its tests.


